## Scope

Hand-written ASCII85Decode filter per PDF spec 7.4.3. Decodes ASCII bytes 0x21..0x75 (! through u) plus the z shortcut into binary bytes. Whitespace ignored. ~> terminator ends the stream.

## Why this matters

ASCII85 is one of the 9 stream-decoder filters required by Phase 1.5; PDFs frequently wrap binary content in ASCII85 to escape it through 7-bit-clean pipelines. Without it, FlateDecode-wrapped-in-ASCII85 streams fail to decode and the document model fails to materialize.

## Implementation guidance

- Decoding algorithm (per PDF spec 7.4.3):
  ```
  let mut accumulator: u32 = 0; let mut count = 0;
  for byte in input {
      if byte == b'~' { check ~> terminator and break }
      if is_whitespace(byte) { continue }
      if byte == b'z' && count == 0 { emit 4 zeros; continue }
      if byte < b'!' || byte > b'u' { STRUCT_INVALID_ASCII85; skip }
      accumulator = accumulator * 85 + (byte - b'!');
      count += 1;
      if count == 5 {
          emit accumulator as 4 big-endian bytes
          accumulator = 0; count = 0;
      }
  }
  // Final partial group: pad with 'u' (84) to 5 bytes, emit count-1 bytes
  if count > 0 {
      for _ in count..5 { accumulator = accumulator * 85 + 84; }
      emit (count-1) bytes from accumulator
  }
  ```
- Accept both <~...~> and bare ...~> forms (the leading <~ is informational)
- Hand-written state machine; no external crate dependency

## Critical considerations

- Whitespace is PDF spec section 7.2.2 (6 bytes: 0x00, 0x09, 0x0A, 0x0C, 0x0D, 0x20); not Rust's char::is_whitespace
- z shortcut MUST only be valid at count == 0 (start of a group); a z mid-group is an error
- Final partial group's padding-with-u trick: if 3 bytes were read, pad to 5 with two 'u' chars (84 each), then emit 3-1=2 of the resulting 4 bytes
- Integer overflow: accumulator * 85 + 84 can exceed u32::MAX for valid input (max 4*256-1 ~ 32-bit) — use saturating or check explicitly; STRUCT_INVALID_ASCII85 on overflow
- ~> terminator MUST be checked; bytes after ~> are ignored
- Bytes outside 0x21..0x75 (except whitespace, z, and ~) emit STRUCT_INVALID_ASCII85 diagnostic but the filter continues (skip the byte)

## Acceptance criteria

- Round-trip: encode 1 KB random bytes via reference ASCII85 encoder, decode → byte-identical
- z shortcut: decoding \"zz\" produces 8 zero bytes
- Odd final group: <~9jqo^~> decodes to specific known bytes (Mary in some test corpora)
- Bytes outside valid range emit STRUCT_INVALID_ASCII85 diagnostic; decoder continues
- Whitespace ignored between digits
- Critical test from plan: <~zNB!c>~> with z shortcut + odd final group decodes correctly

## References

- Plan section: Phase 1.5 Stream Decoder
- PDF spec 7.4.3 ASCII85Decode
- Phase 1.1 STRUCT_INVALID_* diagnostic convention
- Coordinator: pdftract-17rcj (parent — three ASCII filters bundle)
Labels: ascii85, decoder, phase-1

## Scope

Implement FileSource: a PdfSource backed by std::fs::File using plain Read+Seek. The fallback when mmap fails — used for FUSE-mounted filesystems, /proc files, named pipes, and other special files where mmap is not supported.

Module: crates/pdftract-core/src/source/file.rs.

## Why this matters

mmap is the preferred local-file path (sibling MmapSource), but it fails on some valid PDF sources: FUSE mounts where the underlying filesystem doesn't support mmap; /proc/PID/exe-style virtual files; named pipes where length is unknown. FileSource is the safety net that makes \"PDF on the filesystem\" Just Work regardless of fs.

## Implementation guidance

- Module: crates/pdftract-core/src/source/file.rs
- Implementation:
  ```rust
  use std::fs::File;
  use std::io::{self, Read, Seek, SeekFrom};
  use parking_lot::Mutex;
  use bytes::Bytes;

  pub struct FileSource {
      file: Mutex<File>,
      len: u64,
  }

  impl FileSource {
      pub fn open(path: &std::path::Path) -> io::Result<Self> {
          let file = File::open(path)?;
          let len = file.metadata()?.len();
          Ok(Self { file: Mutex::new(file), len })
      }
  }

  impl PdfSource for FileSource {
      fn len(&self) -> u64 { self.len }
      fn read_range(&self, offset: u64, length: usize) -> io::Result<Bytes> {
          let mut file = self.file.lock();
          file.seek(SeekFrom::Start(offset))?;
          let mut buf = vec![0u8; length];
          file.read_exact(&mut buf)?;
          Ok(Bytes::from(buf))
      }
  }
  ```
- parking_lot::Mutex (already used by pdftract-core) for the File handle; std::sync::Mutex is OK too but parking_lot is faster and panics less
- read_range is locking: concurrent reads serialize on the Mutex. This is the cost of seek-based access vs mmap; acceptable for the fallback path
- Read + Seek impls: forward to the locked file (separate cursor state)

## Critical considerations

- The Mutex makes concurrent rayon page-parallelism slower than MmapSource; documented expected behavior
- For named pipes / files of unknown length: len() returns whatever metadata reports (often 0 or 1); parsing may fail when reads return EOF early. Not a panic, just a parse error
- /proc files: contents may change between reads; the PDF parser typically reads once cover-to-cover, so mid-extraction changes produce parse errors which pdftract handles
- FileSource is constructed by the source-dispatch logic when MmapSource::open fails OR when the user explicitly requests it (e.g. via env var PDFTRACT_NO_MMAP=1)
- Bytes::from(Vec<u8>) takes ownership of the heap buffer without copying (one-pass conversion)

## Acceptance criteria

- FileSource::open(/path/to/file.pdf) returns Ok for a valid file
- FileSource::open(/nonexistent) returns Err
- read_range(0, 10) returns the first 10 bytes
- read_range past EOF returns Err (read_exact short-read)
- Send + Sync: FileSource can be sent across threads
- Concurrent read_range from 4 threads on the same source: all succeed (serialized via Mutex)
- Test fixture for a FUSE-mounted file (or sshfs-mounted) verifies fallback path is exercised

