## Scope

Implement MmapSource: a PdfSource backed by memmap2's memory-mapped local file. Applies madvise(MADV_SEQUENTIAL) on content-stream reads to hint the OS for prefetch. Default source for local files when mmap succeeds.

Module: crates/pdftract-core/src/source/mmap.rs (feature-gated: always-on for local files).

## Why this matters

mmap is the fastest local-file access path: zero copies (page-cache-backed mapping appears directly in user address space), low overhead, and the OS handles paging for files larger than RAM. For 100 MB-1 GB PDFs, this is 5-10x faster than read()-based ingestion. Most local-file usage of pdftract goes through MmapSource.

## Implementation guidance

- Crate: memmap2 = \"0.9\"
- Module: crates/pdftract-core/src/source/mmap.rs
- Implementation:
  ```rust
  use memmap2::{Mmap, MmapOptions};
  use bytes::Bytes;

  pub struct MmapSource {
      mmap: Mmap,
      cursor: u64,
  }

  impl MmapSource {
      pub fn open(path: &std::path::Path) -> io::Result<Self> {
          let file = std::fs::File::open(path)?;
          let mmap = unsafe { MmapOptions::new().map(&file)? };
          Ok(Self { mmap, cursor: 0 })
      }
  }

  impl PdfSource for MmapSource {
      fn len(&self) -> u64 { self.mmap.len() as u64 }
      fn read_range(&self, offset: u64, length: usize) -> io::Result<Bytes> {
          let start = offset as usize;
          let end = start.checked_add(length).ok_or(io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::InvalidInput, \"overflow\"))?;
          if end > self.mmap.len() { return Err(io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::UnexpectedEof, \"read past EOF\")); }
          Ok(Bytes::copy_from_slice(&self.mmap[start..end]))
      }
  }
  ```
- MADV_SEQUENTIAL on content streams: when a content stream is being parsed (Phase 3), call self.mmap.advise_range(memmap2::Advice::Sequential, offset, length) to hint the OS
- Read + Seek impls: standard cursor-based forwarding to mmap slice access
- Fallback: if mmap fails (FUSE, /proc, named pipes), the caller (PdfSource::open dispatcher) falls back to FileSource sibling

## Critical considerations

- unsafe { MmapOptions::map(&file) } is unavoidable; memmap2's safety contract documents that the underlying file must not be truncated during the mmap lifetime. pdftract's usage holds the File handle for the mmap lifetime, which is the standard pattern
- Bytes::copy_from_slice copies; for true zero-copy on read_range, consider Bytes::from_static (requires the source to outlive the Bytes, which mmap-backed doesn't guarantee across Drop). Copy-based read_range is acceptable for the simplicity payoff
- Cursor for Read+Seek: a separate field; doesn't affect concurrent read_range calls (read_range is independent of cursor)
- File length is fixed at open time; if the file is truncated externally, subsequent reads beyond the new length will SIGBUS on most platforms. Acceptable behavior (the parse will fail with an io::Error or panic; INV-8 should catch)
- macOS: mmap on certain filesystems (NFS, exotic) may produce inconsistent reads; mmap is best-effort. Fallback to FileSource is the safety net

## Acceptance criteria

- MmapSource::open(/path/to/file.pdf) returns Ok for a valid file
- MmapSource::open(/nonexistent) returns Err
- read_range(0, 10) returns the first 10 bytes
- read_range past EOF returns Err
- len() matches the file size
- Read+Seek trait usage works for serde-based parsers
- Send + Sync: MmapSource can be sent across threads (test with std::thread::spawn)
- MADV_SEQUENTIAL hint compiles and runs without error on Linux/macOS

