## Scope

Implement the --pages RANGE CLI flag accepting comma-separated, 1-based page range specifications. Examples: 1-5, 1,3,7, 12-, 1-5,7,12-. -N (open start) means 1..N inclusive; N- (open end) means N..page_count. Out-of-range emits PAGE_OUT_OF_RANGE diagnostic; skipped (does not abort). Default: all pages. Applies in ALL transport modes (CLI, HTTP serve, MCP, PyO3) and ALL output formats (JSON, NDJSON, text, Markdown).

Module: crates/pdftract-cli/src/cli.rs (flag); crates/pdftract-core/src/pages.rs (range parser).

## Why this matters

The pages flag is the most-used selectivity primitive: users extracting a single chapter from a 500-page PDF want page 47-52, not all 500. For remote PDFs (Phase 1.8) it is even more important — without it, the entire PDF must download. Wiring it through all transports and output formats keeps the user experience consistent regardless of how pdftract was invoked.

## Implementation guidance

- CLI flag (clap derive):
  ```rust
  #[arg(long, value_name = \"RANGE\")]
  pub pages: Option<String>,
  ```
- Range parser:
  ```rust
  pub fn parse_pages(range: &str, page_count: usize) -> Result<BTreeSet<usize>, PageRangeError> {
      let mut indices = BTreeSet::new();
      for part in range.split(',') {
          let part = part.trim();
          if let Some(dash_idx) = part.find('-') {
              let start = &part[..dash_idx];
              let end = &part[dash_idx+1..];
              let s = if start.is_empty() { 1 } else { start.parse()? };
              let e = if end.is_empty() { page_count } else { end.parse()? };
              for n in s..=e {
                  if n > page_count { emit_diagnostic(PAGE_OUT_OF_RANGE, n); continue; }
                  indices.insert(n - 1);  // 1-based to 0-based
              }
          } else {
              let n: usize = part.parse()?;
              if n > page_count { emit_diagnostic(PAGE_OUT_OF_RANGE, n); continue; }
              indices.insert(n - 1);
          }
      }
      Ok(indices)
  }
  ```
- BTreeSet returns sorted, deduplicated 0-based indices
- Apply at the extraction-pipeline level: skip pages not in the set

## Critical considerations

- 1-based input, 0-based internal indices; conversion is explicit
- Page count is known from the page tree (Phase 1.4); the parser must be called AFTER page tree flattening, NOT at CLI parse time
- Empty range -> return all pages (degenerate case; treat as None)
- Range with no page selected after PAGE_OUT_OF_RANGE filtering: don't error; produce empty output (some PDFs may be smaller than expected)
- Open-end N- requires page_count; if invoked before flattening (e.g. for early CLI validation), treat as a deferred check
- Parse error (non-integer, malformed range) returns Err; CLI exits 2 with clap error
- Applies in all transports: HTTP form field pages=1-5, PyO3 pages=1-5, MCP arg pages=1-5

## Acceptance criteria

- parse_pages(1-5, 10) -> BTreeSet {0,1,2,3,4}
- parse_pages(1,3,7, 10) -> BTreeSet {0,2,6}
- parse_pages(12-, 10) -> empty + PAGE_OUT_OF_RANGE diagnostic for 12
- parse_pages(1-5,7,12-15, 10) -> {0,1,2,3,4,6} + diagnostics for 12,13,14,15
- pdftract extract --pages 1-5 file.pdf -> JSON has only pages 0-4
- HTTP serve form field pages=1-5 -> same behavior
- PyO3 extract(path, pages=1-5) -> same behavior
- MCP tools/call extract {pages:1-5} -> same behavior

