## Scope

Implement tokenize_cjk_bytes(codespace: &CodespaceRanges, bytes: &[u8]) -> Vec<u32>. Walks a TJ/Tj byte string and emits a sequence of character codes (each up to 4 bytes wide, packed big-endian into a u32).

Module: crates/pdftract-core/src/cmap/tokenize.rs.

## Why this matters

Without this tokenizer, multi-byte content streams (CJK PDFs, ToUnicode CMaps, custom encodings) are read as a sequence of 1-byte codes producing garbage. The tokenizer is the bridge between the codespace ranges (sibling) and the per-code-point glyph lookup (Phase 3 encoding resolver).

## Implementation guidance

- Algorithm:
  ```
  let mut cursor = 0;
  let mut codes = Vec::with_capacity(bytes.len()); // preallocate
  while cursor < bytes.len() {
      // Try widest first (per ISO 32000-1 9.10.3.1)
      let mut matched = false;
      for width in [4u8, 3, 2, 1] {
          if cursor + (width as usize) > bytes.len() { continue; }
          for range in &codespace.ranges {
              if range.width != width { continue; }
              let candidate = &bytes[cursor..cursor + width as usize];
              if (0..width as usize).all(|i| candidate[i] >= range.lo[i] && candidate[i] <= range.hi[i]) {
                  // Pack big-endian into u32
                  let mut code = 0u32;
                  for &b in candidate { code = (code << 8) | b as u32; }
                  codes.push(code);
                  cursor += width as usize;
                  matched = true;
                  break;
              }
          }
          if matched { break; }
      }
      if !matched {
          // Unrecognized byte; emit U+FFFD code and advance 1
          codes.push(0xFFFD);
          emit_diagnostic_once(CJK_TOKENIZE_UNKNOWN_BYTE, bytes[cursor]);
          cursor += 1;
      }
  }
  ```
- Preallocate codes Vec (bytes.len() upper bound for 1-byte codes)
- Emit CJK_TOKENIZE_UNKNOWN_BYTE diagnostic ONCE per (font, byte_value) — flood prevention via a per-call HashSet of seen byte values

## Critical considerations

- Widest-first match per ISO 32000-1 9.10.3.1 — re-read the spec to confirm; some implementations use shortest-first. The spec wording: \"To determine the length of an encoded byte sequence, the lengths of the codespace ranges are examined, and the byte sequence is determined to have the same length as the longest matching range.\" Widest-first
- Range matching is per-byte: candidate[i] must be in [range.lo[i], range.hi[i]] for ALL bytes i in the width
- Some CMaps overlap (e.g. 0x80 in both 1-byte and 2-byte lead ranges); widest-first resolves this
- Empty codespace (no ranges): defaults to single-byte 0x00-0xFF (every byte is a 1-byte code); document in module doc
- Hot path: avoid allocation per code (preallocate Vec); inner loop is iterated once per byte; benchmark with a CJK 100-page document to verify < 10 ms total tokenization
- The output u32 is the byte-packed code; downstream CMap lookup (beginbfchar / begincidrange) consumes this

## Acceptance criteria

- Input ASCII bytes 0x48 0x65 0x6C 0x6C 0x6F with codespace <00><7F> → codes [0x48, 0x65, 0x6C, 0x6C, 0x6F]
- Input 2-byte CJK 0x82 0xA0 with codespace <8000><FFFF> → codes [0x82A0]
- Mixed 1+2 byte input: 0x48 0x82 0xA0 with codespace <00><7F><8000><FFFF> → [0x48, 0x82A0]
- Unrecognized byte (no matching range): emit U+FFFD code + CJK_TOKENIZE_UNKNOWN_BYTE diagnostic once
- Empty codespace defaults to 1-byte 0x00-0xFF coverage
- Regression test for the widest-first vs shortest-first edge case (0x80 in both 1-byte and 2-byte lead range)
- Benchmark: 100 KB CJK content stream tokenized in < 10 ms

