## Scope

Implement the 9 contract methods plus support primitives as extern \"C\" functions in crates/pdftract-libpdftract/src/api.rs. The API returns owned JSON strings the caller frees with pdftract_free(); streaming uses opaque handles with open/next/close ops. All functions are reentrant and thread-safe.

## Why this matters

The 9 methods are the SDK conformance surface (per the SDK contract spec, sibling bead pdftract-147a). The owned-string convention (\"caller frees\") is the standard C-FFI pattern; the streaming handle pattern (open/next/close) avoids streaming JSON over an FFI boundary in one call (which would require buffering the entire stream in memory).

## Implementation guidance

### Method signatures

```c
// Returns JSON string; caller MUST free with pdftract_free()
char* pdftract_extract(const char* source, const char* options_json);
char* pdftract_extract_text(const char* source, const char* options_json);
char* pdftract_extract_markdown(const char* source, const char* options_json);

// Streaming returns an opaque handle; pdftract_stream_next yields one Page JSON at a time
void* pdftract_extract_stream_open(const char* source, const char* options_json);
char* pdftract_stream_next(void* handle);  // returns NULL when stream ends
void pdftract_stream_close(void* handle);

char* pdftract_search(const char* source, const char* pattern, const char* options_json);
char* pdftract_get_metadata(const char* source, const char* options_json);
char* pdftract_hash(const char* source);
char* pdftract_classify(const char* source);

// Memory management
void pdftract_free(char* ptr);

// Versioning
const char* pdftract_version(void);  // static string; do NOT free
```

### Implementation pattern

```rust
#[no_mangle]
pub extern \"C\" fn pdftract_extract(source: *const c_char, options_json: *const c_char) -> *mut c_char {
    let result = std::panic::catch_unwind(|| {
        let source = unsafe { CStr::from_ptr(source) }.to_str()?;
        let options: ExtractionOptions = serde_json::from_str(unsafe { CStr::from_ptr(options_json) }.to_str()?)?;
        let doc = pdftract_core::extract(source, &options)?;
        Ok::<_, anyhow::Error>(serde_json::to_string(&doc)?)
    });
    match result {
        Ok(Ok(s)) => CString::new(s).unwrap().into_raw(),
        Ok(Err(e)) => CString::new(json_error(e)).unwrap().into_raw(),
        Err(_) => CString::new(json_error(\"panic in pdftract_extract\")).unwrap().into_raw(),
    }
}

#[no_mangle]
pub extern \"C\" fn pdftract_free(ptr: *mut c_char) {
    if ptr.is_null() { return; }
    unsafe { drop(CString::from_raw(ptr)) }
}
```

- catch_unwind around every entry point (panics over FFI boundaries are UB; convert to JSON errors)
- Error JSON shape: {\"error\":\"CODE\",\"message\":\"...\"} — matches the SDK contract (sibling pdftract-147a)
- Streaming handle: Box<StreamState> cast to *mut c_void; stream_next pops next page; stream_close drops the box

## Critical considerations

- INV-8 (no panic across FFI boundary): every extern \"C\" function MUST wrap its body in catch_unwind; bare panics over FFI are undefined behavior in Rust
- Memory ownership: pdftract_extract returns a CString::into_raw pointer; caller MUST free via pdftract_free. Calling free() (libc) on the pointer corrupts the allocator state (pdftract uses Rust's allocator)
- The stream handle is opaque (void*); the consumer never dereferences it. Internally it's a Box<StreamState>; ownership is moved into the open call and out of the close call
- Thread safety: pdftract-core extraction is already thread-safe (no shared mutable state); the extern \"C\" wrappers don't add any synchronization
- Null pointer arguments: every entry point checks for null source / null options_json and returns an error JSON; never deref a null pointer
- Invalid UTF-8 in source/options_json: CStr::to_str returns Err; convert to error JSON
- pdftract_version returns a static C string (no free); rest of API returns owned strings

## Acceptance criteria

- 12 exported symbols on libpdftract.so (verified via nm -D | grep pdftract_): extract, extract_text, extract_markdown, extract_stream_open, stream_next, stream_close, search, get_metadata, hash, classify, free, version
- Sample C client program calls pdftract_extract on a fixture PDF, parses returned JSON, free()s
- Sample C++ client uses the same header (cpp_compat)
- Null source/options → error JSON, not crash
- Panic in extraction (e.g. malformed PDF triggering an internal panic in a future bug) → error JSON, not crash; catch_unwind verified
- Memory roundtrip: alloc + free 10,000 times → no leak (verified with valgrind in CI)
- Thread safety: 8 concurrent extract calls from different pthreads → all succeed; no data races (TSan)

