## Scope

Implement pdftract.extract_stream(path, **kwargs) -> Iterator[dict] as a PyO3 function. Returns a custom StreamIterator PyClass whose __next__ method yields one Page dict per call, driving the underlying NDJSON streaming pipeline incrementally.

Module: crates/pdftract-py/src/extract_stream.rs.

## Why this matters

Streaming extraction is the memory-efficient mode for large PDFs (1000+ pages); without it, Python users would have to load the entire Output dict into memory at once. The iterator protocol is the idiomatic Python interface for incremental data; users consume it with for page in pdftract.extract_stream(...).

## Implementation guidance

- StreamIterator PyClass:
  ```rust
  #[pyclass]
  pub struct StreamIterator {
      receiver: Option<std::sync::mpsc::Receiver<PageFrame>>,
      handle: Option<std::thread::JoinHandle<Result<(), PdftractError>>>,
  }

  #[pymethods]
  impl StreamIterator {
      fn __iter__(slf: PyRef<Self>) -> PyRef<Self> { slf }
      fn __next__(&mut self, py: Python<'_>) -> PyResult<Option<PyObject>> {
          let recv = self.receiver.as_ref().ok_or(PyStopIteration::new_err(()))?;
          let frame = py.allow_threads(|| recv.recv());
          match frame {
              Ok(page_frame) => Ok(Some(pythonize::pythonize(py, &page_frame)?)),
              Err(_) => {
                  // Channel closed: thread done; check for error
                  if let Some(h) = self.handle.take() { h.join().unwrap()?; }
                  Err(PyStopIteration::new_err(()))
              }
          }
      }
  }

  #[pyfunction]
  pub fn extract_stream(py: Python<'_>, path: &str, kwargs: Option<&PyDict>) -> PyResult<Py<StreamIterator>> {
      let opts = parse_kwargs(kwargs)?;
      let (tx, rx) = std::sync::mpsc::channel();
      let path_owned = path.to_string();
      let handle = std::thread::spawn(move || {
          pdftract_core::extract_stream(&path_owned, &opts, |page| tx.send(page).is_ok())
      });
      Py::new(py, StreamIterator { receiver: Some(rx), handle: Some(handle) })
  }
  ```
- yields ONLY page dicts (frame: \"page\" equivalent); NOT header / footer (per plan line 2067)
- GIL released during recv (py.allow_threads)
- Errors from the background thread propagate via h.join().unwrap()? after channel closes

## Critical considerations

- The background thread + mpsc channel is the standard pattern for adapting Rust callbacks to Python iterators
- GIL release on recv is mandatory; otherwise the background extraction thread can't proceed (it needs to acquire GIL to do nothing in Python, which is paradoxical — the GIL is released so Python's GC doesn't block)
- StopIteration is raised when the channel closes; this signals end-of-extraction
- A panic in the background thread: h.join() returns Err; convert to PdftractError
- Drop impl on StreamIterator must clean up: drop receiver (closes channel), join handle (waits for thread)

## Acceptance criteria

- for page in pdftract.extract_stream(\"file.pdf\"): print(page[\"page_index\"]) iterates correctly
- Each yielded dict has the Page schema (page_index, spans, etc.)
- StopIteration raised at end-of-stream
- Error in extraction propagates as PdftractError raised from __next__
- Memory: streaming a 1000-page PDF uses < 100 MB Python memory (vs. multi-GB for non-streaming)

